Date: Sun, 26 Sep 93 2:50:51 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: BASICS: Death

In life engineering, as in software engineering, it is often
valuable to look at the edge cases.  So when can you truly be called 
"dead"?  When some nurse or coroner pronounces you "dead", is 
this a magick incantation, causing your soul to flee from your 
body, never to return?  Extropians heavily doubt it.

All "death" has ever been is that state of unconsciousness due to 
progressive damage deemed irreversible by current technology.  
Once it was cessation of breathing; up until the 1950s it was 
cessation of heartbeat, now it's cessation of certain kinds of 
brain signals.  When you are declared "clinically
dead" most of your brain cells are still alive, long-term memory is
usually intact, etc.  The real ultimate death, the destruction of 
the long-term memory and other structures that define "you",
occurs while you are lying in the morgue.  As medical tech
advances we will move away from historical process
definitions of death to structural definitions: how much of
your synaptic connections and other long term memory structures 
are still intact? 

You are often still "you" with substantial long-term memory loss, 
but some of the most horrible diseases are those where 
most personality and long-term memory is destroyed while the
body lives on, eg Alzheimer's.  On the flip side, many of
the most valuable processes of civilization, for example education,
serve to build and improve the personality instead of destroy it like 
Alzheimer's.  Such improvement Extropians call self-transformation, and 
we have discovered that our potential for expanding our
consciousness, like our potential for cheating death,
is practically limitless: not because of convenient magickal
incantations handed down from on high, but because we make the
most of exploring this world building our futures in it.

