Apart from the two references already posted:

     "Quantum Theory, the Church-Turing principle and the universal
     quantum computer" by David Deutsch, Proceedings of the Royal
     Society of London A 400 97-117 (1985)
and 
     "Quantum computational networks" also by Deutsch, Proceedings
     of the Royal Society of London A 425 73-90 (1989)

there is also some work by R(?) Josza (sp?).  Unfortunately I don't have
any precise refs for him.

Quantum computers have three properties that (would) distinguish them
from conventional computers:
a)   they can generate random (rather than pseudo-random) numbers
b)   they produce quantum correlated output states (eg for quantum
     cryptography)
c)   they can perform quantum parallelism - although this can't be used
     to out-compute normal computers except in highly artifical, rigged
     circumstances

