Subject: PPL: Lloyds and crypto-anarchical insurance

X-reposting-policy:  Repost only with permission.  (C) 1993, Tim Hruby.

Nick sez:

> By quantifying and spreading risk more widely, catastrophe futures
> might also make possible new kinds of products, eg insurance against
> damage by war, terrorism, riot, confiscatory taxation, forfeiture,
> criminal penalties, etc. none of which can currently be insured
> against (that I'm aware of) because of uncertain statistical profile,
> very high potential damage claims, illegality in some jurisdictions,
> and in some cases moral hazard.  Tax insurance in particular could
> become a major driving force of crypto-anarchy.
> 
> Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

Au contraire, quite a few of these things can be insured against.  War, civil
unrest, and expropriation insurance are all available on the insurance 
markets.  Expropriation insurance generally includes a "creeping
expropriation" clause.  This allows one to collect when one deems that
increased government regulation and taxation of one's business has risen to
the level that it is 
no longer profitable/possible for one to continue operating -- the catch that
keeps you honest is that to collect you have to sign over the remains of your
business to the insurance company.  Expropriation policies are tricky of
course,
as the cost a lot more if you want coverage vs. *any* colorable
expropriation,
rather than simply those expropriations considered "illegitimate" under the
general commercial/international law usage. 

Typically, these policies can be bought from your friendly statist export
promotion agency.  In the case of some nations, like, oh... say, France, the
agency is subsidized by the government, and thus charges less than market
rates.
However, in some countries, like the U.S., the agency (the Overseas Private
Investment Corporation, or OPIC) is required to fund itself, and thus must 
charge at least "break even" rates.  These government services are generally
available only to their nationals who are investing abroad.  However, the 
government insurers are not the only players.  International organizations 
like the World Bank have similar insurance companies (MIGA, in the case of 
the World Bank -- off the top of my head, I can't recall if MIGA is
subsidized
or not).  In spite of all this government interference in the market, private
insurance policies are also available, with most of the business being done
through Lloyd's and other major reinsurance syndicates.

Another example, and one that comes very close to the private-protection 
agencies envisioned under a PPL system, is the market in kidnapping
insurance.
Most multi-national corporations doing business in "kidnapping prone" regions

of the globe (such as Latin America) carry kidnapping policies on their most
highly valued employees.  Which employees are considered "most highly valued"
is often kept secret, for reasons of corporate morale.  These policies can 
be written a number of ways, depending on the MNC's preferences for deterring

and resolving kidnaps.  The more expensive policy might require the insurance
company to simply pay up whenever there is a real kidnapping occuring -- this
policy is more expensive because of a number of moral hazards this places on 
the MNC.  The less expensive policy might turn over most responsibility for
resolving the kidnapping to the insurance company, who then has some leeway 
in deciding whether to simply pay up, or hire an extraction team (like the 
team that G. Gordon Liddy owns) to effect a more cost-effective rescue.  
Before anyone asks, I'm sure there are provisions built into this type of 
policy to mitigate the moral hazard this places on the insurance company.

How does this all relate to crypto-anarchy? Well, if you are a foreign 
national planning on making a business investment in the U.S., you probably 
can get the types of insurance mentioned above (war, civil unrest, liability 
in civil suits, expropriation, etc.).  It may not even cost that much, given 
the past stability of the U.S. system (this observation -- stabilty or cost
--
doesn't apply to the tort system, where the rules have been in considerable 
flux for some time now).  If you are a U.S. national, you can probably get
coverage, either personal or business, for your U.S. activities through the
private international reinsurers.  Be prepared to pay a large commission,
however, as this is an uncommon insurance transaction, and therefore will
have
large transactions costs due to the lack of any economies of scale having
built up.

International insurance claims are generally adjudicated and enforced through
international arbitration procedures, thus bypassing national laws, as the
parties typically pick both the arbitrators and the law governing the claim 
(i.e., the epitome of a PPL system -- in fact, one generally finds that
private international law, lacking a coercive Leviathan, embodies the PPL
aproach to law).  Negotiating over a choice of judges and law acceptable to
an
Extropian 
is likely to increase both the negotiating costs, as well as the final
premium
the insurer is willing to charge, so be prepared to pay for this as well. 
Generally, large international insurers and reinsurers will pay off
arbitration awards, due to the reputation repercussions they would face if
they ignored 
them.  However, enforcement of arbitration awards *can* be had through
national courts, and if the nation is a party to the New York Convention on
Foreign Arbital Awards (or some similar name) generally the court enforces
without 
regard to the underlying nature of the contract, law, etc.  (Seek competent 
legal advice -- your milage may vary, depending on the provisions you wind up
with). 

**BUSINESS OPPORTUNITY**
If crypto-anarchy and the BlackNet (tm) pick up, and a market does develop 
where people seek this kind of insurance protection, a start-up company could
make a lot of money by acting as the middleman between individuals and the
international reinsurers.  However, that said, a lot of work remains to be 
done on developing actuarial table for such policies, analysis of the moral
hazards and possible solutions involved in such contracts, and choice of law 
to govern disputes.  

In areas such as "tax insurance," insurance may not be the most efficent 
solution to the problem -- offshore mutual funds designed with "tax avoidance"

in mind, and a sufficient incentive for the manager to perform well, may well

be a much better method of keeping one's money out of the government's hands.

The moral hazards of tax insurance schemes (e.g., why risk coercive and 
punitive sanctions from the state for tax evasion, when you can just pay, and
know that the insurance company will reimburse you) might well result in the
premiums approaching the amount of the tax paid, i.e. no risk spreading at
all.
That is, the more efficient solution to the tax problem may well be to pay
someone to keep your money out of the government's hands, rather than bet
that
the government won't find it.  Of course, such a mutual fund may well be more
attractive to investors if it is insured against government interference/
seizure, so I guess there is an incentive to solve the moral hazard problem. 
Still, it may be more efficient to solve it/monitor it among the specialists/
sophisticated actors, i.e., the fund managers are monitored in some way by 
the insurers, rather than have the insurers try to monitor every individual 
who wants to avoid taxes.  

