James Donald:

> But in a posting elsewhere [Price] committed himself to the
> nicely simplistic definition given in Webster's dictionary

True.

> Webster's definition:  The view that experimental
> operations must define scientific terms and concepts.

Donald, with his usual lack of precision and understanding, has got this
the wrong way around.  Webster's said that scientific concepts are
defined by experiment.  It says nothing about where you dream up new
ideas, nor whether you believe in reality.  It doesn't say, (as I
previously said) that you must have your head stuck up a spectroscope. 
You can be stoned out in a Tibetan monastery, suffering from hayfever
on Heligoland or dying of fever in a Prussian WW-I military hospital
when insight dawns.  It doesn't matter.  Donald's inability to grasp
this renders everything else he says worthless.

> you cannot give an operational definition of a dog.

Well _you_ can't, maybe.  Let's hear your definition (a dog is a dog?)
and we'll all have a good laugh.

> most [great scientists] were ... realists

Me too.   (Note for Donald's edification, since his logical faculties
          are sadly deficient:  I am not a GS)

> James A. Donald

