Re: Max More's search for a biologist for the Foundation movie.

In my work for my CD-ROM project, "RU Sirius' Scrapbook of the Future or
How to Mutate and Take over the World", I do research on what we term TASM
(Technologically Assisted Self-Mutation). I don't have a lot on it yet, but
here is a relevant item:

>From the TASM (physical) clippings file:
Begin Clipping
________________________________________
Brave New Health Care
By Jack Fischer
Mercury News Staff writer
San Jose Mercury News, date unknown.

        Washington - Thanks to predicted scientific advances, a victim of
health disease might someday opt to grow a second heart in his or her chest
that surgeons would then use to replace the diseased heart.
...
        Such are the visions of Clement Bezold, a political scientist with
extensive experience as a consultant to major pharmaceutical companies and
government agencies on the future of health care.

Future health care

        Bezold was one of a half dozen experts gathered in Washington on
Monday to discuss health care in the coming decades at the World Futures
Society's seventh general assembly.
        Bezold's forecasts were just a sample of medical advances experts
discussed, including such changes as the advent of customized drugs attuned
to the patient's unique biochemistry, and software that would enable direct
transmission of patient's vital signs to doctors.
...
        In fact, Henry Aaron, director of economic studies at the Brookings
Institution and also another health care expert, told another gathering at
the assambly that advances in biomedical research would dwarf changes
wrought by the harnessing of the atom and the advent of the computer.

Clearing arterial plaque

        Bezold, who is director of the Institute for Alternative Futures in
Alexandria, VA, said his forecast in sharp reduction in heart disease was
based on what he called a "Drano Pill", now in development by several major
pharmaceutical companies, that will clear plaque from the circulatory
system.
...
        Another health care futurist, Jonathan Peck, also with the
Institute for Alternative Futures, said the changes in health care wrought
by the baby boom generation as it begins to grow old could be as
significant as those brought by the advances in science.
        Peck said that the increased availability of medical information to
patients, combined with the boomers' distrust of institutions, could mean a
shift responsibility for medical decisions from doctors to patients.
...
        Further off, several decades into the future, Bezold looks forward
to the day when nanotechnology - the science of engineering microscopic
devices - makes it possible for a patient to swallow a device that would
monitor blood chemistry and make adjustments to them on the spot.
...
_______________________________________________________________________
End Clipping

None of these people are specifically listed as biologists (actually
everyone seems to be in economics, I always thought my health care was
economically determined <g>), but Clement Bezold and his Institute for
Alternative Futures seems like a good place to start. Jonathan Peck is
predicting (and, I assume, promoting) a health care system that allows more
patient freedom, good for extropians. 

BTW - I'd be interested in talking to anyone you come up with, Max.

